{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794480853,"nonce":"3TsL72N8FHi0EiWAJlcjjhn25yQ=","message":"Windowed repro and the fixes it forced, local in b604efc43937db2bf5e2aa35c77d3a2c8e592aa1 (not pushed).\n\nexamples/blinc_app_examples/examples/node_table_repro.rs shows nine tile kinds in a window (translate, scale, turn, nested, all four, a list of bound rows, text only; FALLBACKS=1 adds a fade with text and a clipped card). Run it with and without BLINC_NODE_TABLE=1 and compare. In a real window with the table on, every animated frame reports rows applied.\n\nWhat the window showed that the harness did not\n- The cache paint (the compositor's inner paint into the static layer) dispatches motion text unconditionally, and the compositor draws it again on top. With the table the static copy stays at the pose of the paint while the rows move the other, so a label stays behind while its card moves. It is hidden while the moved card still covers it, which is why single-card scenes passed. Where motion is not baked to textures the inner dispatch is skipped now.\n- A node that moves only text had no binding record (the walker records only a non-empty range), so the table never saw it and its text stayed put. It has one now, and a slot. Its slot range was kept as 0..0 while the record said 2..2, so the slots never matched, the table re-planned every frame and fell back to a full paint for the whole frame; fixed.\n- A binding settled where it was painted lives in the static batch and used to block the table for every other binding; it is left alone now unless its value has moved.\n- A frame holding anything the table cannot move (an opacity group with text, a clip) is painted in full for every tile on screen, not only that tile. The compositor's patch result is all or nothing. Moving to per-binding fallback is the next structural step.\n- The harness reference painted the label of a text-only node twice (same cause as the first point), so it was heavier than a single draw; the no-promotion paint skips the duplicate now.\n\nMeasured, not seen: label ink while a card zooms 1x to 2.5x at density 1 and 2 grows the same in the vector paint and the table (no fade); the promoted fast path keeps it flat (the frozen label).","files":null}]}